    "A18": {
      "worksheet": "Introduction",
      "xmlPath": "xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml",
      "cell": "A18",
      "value": "●  Scope 2 (energy indirect) emissions are those released into the atmosphere that are associated with your consumption of purchased electricity, heat, steam and cooling. These indirect emissions are a consequence of your organisation’s energy use, but occur at sources you do not own or control.",
      "rawValue": "17",
      "type": "s",
      "numberFormat": "General",
      "styleId": 469,
      "formula": null
    },
    "A20": {
      "worksheet": "Introduction",
      "xmlPath": "xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml",
      "cell": "A20",
      "value": "The organisation should decide if they are going to include scope 3 Well To Tank (WTT) emissions within their reporting.  This should be applied consistently across all fuel sources and activities.   i.e. Organisation A has elected to include WTT within its reporting so when calculating emissions from their electricity consumption they should account for:\nElectricity (UK Grid Generated) kWh Consumed = Scope 2\nElectricity (UK Grid Generated) associated T&D = Scope 3\nElectricity (UK Grid Generated) associated generation WTT = Scope 3\nElectricity (UK Grid Generated) associated T&D WTT = Scope 3\n",
      "rawValue": "18",
      "type": "s",
      "numberFormat": "General",
      "styleId": 469,
      "formula": null
    },
    "A29": {
      "worksheet": "Introduction",
      "xmlPath": "xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml",
      "cell": "A29",
      "value": "Navigate to the sheet relating to the activity that you wish to calculate emissions for. Read the guidance and then collect or estimate activity data for your organisation (for example, the amount of electricity used or distance travelled). Then multiply this activity data by the relevant (emission) conversion factor. This gives an estimate of the GHG emissions for that activity.",
      "rawValue": "26",
      "type": "s",
      "numberFormat": "General",
      "styleId": 455,
      "formula": null
    },
    "A34": {
      "worksheet": "Introduction",
      "xmlPath": "xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml",
      "cell": "A34",
      "value": "All conversion factors presented here are in units of 'kilograms of carbon dioxide equivalent of Y per X' (kg CO2e of Y per X), where Y is the gas emitted and X is the unit activity.  CO2e is the universal unit of measurement to indicate the global warming potential (GWP) of GHGs, expressed in terms of the GWP of one unit of carbon dioxide. ",
      "rawValue": "1730",
      "type": "s",
      "numberFormat": "General",
      "styleId": 455,
      "formula": null
    },
    "A35": {
      "worksheet": "Introduction",
      "xmlPath": "xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml",
      "cell": "A35",
      "value": "The GWPs used in the calculation of CO2e are based on the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) Fifth Assessment Report (AR5) over a 100-year period so that the Conversion Factors are consistent with current national and international reporting requirements. ",
      "rawValue": "30",
      "type": "s",
      "numberFormat": "General",
      "styleId": 455,
      "formula": null
    },
    "A38": {
      "worksheet": "Introduction",
      "xmlPath": "xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml",
      "cell": "A38",
      "value": "You should use the factors that correlates with the activity data on which you are reporting (for example, factors labelled as 2026 should be used for data from calendar year 2026). One of the following two approaches may be adopted:\n1) The 'Majority' approach -  If you are reporting on an April to March year, the factors from the calendar year in which the greatest portion of your data falls should be applied (for example, the 2026 factors should be applied to data in reporting year 01/04/26 – 31/03/27, the 2025 factors should have been applied to data in reporting year 01/04/25 – 31/03/26). Users that operate a July to June reporting year should apply the newest set of available factors.\n2) The 'Blended' approach - That uses the specific number of days that fall within each conversion factor period. i.e. if you have 220 days in the 2025 conversion factor period and 145 days in the 2026 conversion factor period these proportions are applied to the blending of the two periods' conversion factor as long as this is transparently stated (days blending split), auditable and consistently applied.\nThe approach utilised should be clearly stated and consistently applied.",
      "rawValue": "1739",
      "type": "s",
      "numberFormat": "General",
      "styleId": 467,
      "formula": null
    }
  },
